WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Narrator] The last set
of rights we'll discuss in

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:07.410
this lesson are the
economic rights of citizens.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.740
These are the rights that
citizens have to control their own

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.560
property, labor and working conditions.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.610
This includes all of the rights
associated with your ability

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.253
to make money and your
ability to own stuff.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.680
The most basic of these rights is

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.680
to acquire, use, transfer
and dispose off property.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.970
That is you can buy stuff, you can use it,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.400
you can give it or sell it to someone else

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.700
and you can throw it away.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.480
These property rights also
include your right to receive

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.390
just compensation for the
taking of private property for

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.820
public use, which is protected
by the fifth amendment.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.740
So if the government decides
that they need your yard,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.560
because they're building a highway,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.580
they must compensate you
fairly for that lands.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.930
The government also protects
your rights to copyright

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.710
and patent so that you can sue someone

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.770
who steals your ideas, work or inventions.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.740
So this includes your right
to own things that aren't

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.313
physical objects or lands,
like a song you wrote.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:21.260
Then there are the economic
rights that safeguard your

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.000
freedom to own your own labor

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.130
and the money you earn from it.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.520
Those include the right to earn a wage,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.420
the right to choose your own
work and change your employment

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.080
and the right to work in safe conditions.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.680
Lastly, you have economic
rights to associate with

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.930
and combine your interests with others.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.520
Such as the right to join
labor unions and professional

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.180
associations to advocate for yourself

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.240
and for other members of your profession.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.600
You can establish and operate a business

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.620
and enter into lawful
contracts with others.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.580
The ability to own one's own property,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.740
labor and business dealings
is foundational to the system

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.670
of capitalism, in which
individuals control economic

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.290
organizations rather than
the state or co-operatives.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.840
So now let's do a couple of scenarios,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.020
so you can check your skills.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.570
I'll give you a scenario and
you see if you can identify

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:26.780
which economic right is
being exercised or violated.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.410
Number one,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.110
Yael and a group of her
coworkers are frustrated

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.330
with the working conditions
at the grocery store

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.310
they work at.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.980
They're forced to work 10 hours days

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.500
with only a 30 minute break for lunch.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.270
Several of her co-workers have started

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:43.450
reporting knee problems because
they aren't allowed to sit.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.810
Yael gathers a majority
of employees to talk

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.860
about organizing a union
to demand less hours

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:49.960
and more breaks.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.950
Her boss hears about the
meeting and fires Yael.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:57.193
Which right, did her employer
violate in this scenario?

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.010
The right to join a labor union.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.800
Yael has the right to join unions

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.150
or professional
associations as she chooses.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.630
Number two,

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.250
one day, a city council
member knocks on Tai's door.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.300
He tells Tai that the city
needs half of his backyard

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.200
to expand the public park that sits

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.330
on the other side of Tai's fence.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.540
Tai refuses to give the land away,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.480
but the city council member tells Tai

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.610
he doesn't have a choice

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.594
and that he will receive a cheque for $50

00:03:26.594 --> 00:03:30.270
to repair the fence once
they take it down tomorrow.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.783
Which right did the city council
violate in this scenario?

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:38.610
The right to just
compensation for the taking

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.770
of private property for public use.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.960
Tai's back yard is worth
a lot more than $50.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.300
Number three,

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.310
Hamza's boss calls him
into his office one day

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.820
and lets him know that
there's a new opening

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.930
in another department in the company.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.170
But Hamza doesn't want to move departments

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.190
and enjoys the job he has.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:00.760
He requests not to change.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.890
His boss tells him that if he doesn't move

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:05.500
to the new department,
she'll have to fire him.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.800
Instead, Hamza quits.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.180
He starts looking for
a new job immediately.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.640
Which right did Hamza
use in this scenario?

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.410
He used his right to change employment.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.243
Okay.

00:04:19.243 --> 00:04:20.390
That's all for this video.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.720
What do you think is the most
important economic right?

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.350
What would be the consequences

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.803
if you didn't have one of these rights?

